ref('ginzburg1983symplectic','Ginzburg, Viktor A.','Symplectic geometry and representations',1983,'https://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=faa&option_lang=rus&paperid=1562&what=fullt',journal='Functional Analysis and Its Applications',volume=17,number=3,pages='225--227',doi='10.1007/BF01078110',annote='Math-Net metadata and full two-page Russian original, 17(3),75--76, opened and read, especially Theorems 1--5. Received 23 December 1982. Reference 3 explicitly identifies a 1981 Moscow State University preprint with the same title, not itself located. The short paper does not state the notebook converse about arbitrary idempotent canonical relations.')
cumulative('hormander1971fourier','Full original reopened and Theorems 4.2.2--4.2.3, pages178--181, read: transverse composition requires properness and proper support. General clean-excess extension is not claimed to be this theorem.')
ref('homanzhou2017radon','Homan, Andrew and Zhou, Hanming','Injectivity and Stability for a Generic Class of Generalized Radon Transforms',2017,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12220-016-9729-4',journal='Journal of Geometric Analysis',volume=27,pages='1515--1529',doi='10.1007/s12220-016-9729-4',annote='Full publisher open-access text read, introduction and Sections2,4. Published online30June2016, issue2017. Historical attribution of the Bolker normal-operator result to Guillemin--Sternberg is indirect evidence for priority; the modern paper explicitly verifies its own hypotheses and symbol calculus.')
talk(570,'1982-10-11','V. Ginzburg (catalogue: V. L.; mathematical identification: V. A.)','P001049-P001052','Coisotropic reduction, canonical projectors and double fibrations',r'The actual entry occupies P001050 and the upper part of P001051, before Fröhlich. The notebook says only V. Ginzburg and explicitly presents the material after Guillemin--Sternberg. The mathematical subject and nearby publications point to Viktor A. Ginzburg; the catalogue\textquotesingle s V. L. is retained as a provenance discrepancy. The talk relates a regular coisotropic quotient to a symmetric idempotent canonical relation and an operator algebra, then Shmelyov gives the conormal embedding condition for a double fibration.')
add(570,'The characteristic foliation and regular symplectic reduction','P001050','construction',r'\(X\) is complex, \(\Sigma\subset T^*X\) is conic and coisotropic, and \(\Sigma\to X\) is a fibration. The kernel of the restricted symplectic form is said to be integrable; its quotient \(M\) is assumed to be a fibration.',r'For a smooth coisotropic \(\Sigma\) in a symplectic manifold \((P,\omega)\), \(\ker(\omega|_\Sigma)=(T\Sigma)^\omega\) is integrable. If its leaf space \(M\) is smooth and the quotient \(q:\Sigma\to M\) is a surjective submersion with connected fibers, there is a unique symplectic form \(\omega_M\) satisfying \(q^*\omega_M=\omega|_\Sigma\). Closed leaves alone do not guarantee a smooth Hausdorff quotient.',r'The notes expressly attribute the framework to earlier Guillemin--Sternberg work. \citet[Theorem~1]{ginzburg1983symplectic} uses the same characteristic reduction; its reference~3 records the author\textquotesingle s 1981 preprint. The 1983 publication date therefore cannot establish novelty in October 1982.',('ginzburg1983symplectic',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='Smoothness and the quotient-submersion hypothesis are made explicit; the closed-form/Frobenius argument is the standard qualification.')
add(570,'The Lagrangian equivalence relation of a coisotropic quotient','P001050','construction',r'\(\Lambda=\{(\xi_1,\xi_2)\in\Sigma^2:\xi_1\sim\xi_2\}\) is Lagrangian and satisfies \(\Lambda^t=\Lambda\), \(\Lambda\circ\Lambda=\Lambda\).',r'With the preceding regular quotient, \(\Lambda=\Sigma\times_M\Sigma\) is Lagrangian in \(P\times\overline P\), where the second symplectic form is \(-\omega\). If \(\dim P=2n\) and \(\operatorname{codim}\Sigma=k\), then \(\dim\Lambda=2n\); equality of the two pullbacks of \(q^*\omega_M\) gives isotropy. Symmetry and idempotence are the ordinary equivalence-relation identities.',r'This is the canonical-relation form of the reduction used in \citet[Section~2]{ginzburg1983symplectic}. The dimension and pullback check above is supplied explicitly \ours; the source does not present it as a new 1983 theorem.',('ginzburg1983symplectic',),verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The reversed symplectic sign and the regular fiber product are essential additions.')
add(570,'The proposed converse for symmetric idempotent relations','P001051','conjecture',r'\ru{По-видимому, обратно}: a Lagrangian \(\Lambda\) with \(\Lambda^t=\Lambda\) and \(\Lambda\circ\Lambda=\Lambda\) should arise from \(\Sigma\).',r'Under regularity assumptions on both projections, a smooth support \(\Sigma\), a smooth quotient and connected equivalence classes, the relation is the characteristic-leaf relation of a coisotropic support. The notebook does not say whether singular supports, immersed relations, disconnected classes or non-Hausdorff quotients are allowed, so an unrestricted global converse is not a uniquely specified assertion.',r'\citet{ginzburg1983symplectic} gives a related coisotropic realization theorem for Lagrangian families, not the unrestricted converse written here. Searches for canonical projectors and symmetric idempotent relations did not identify an exact source matching all omitted global choices.',('ginzburg1983symplectic',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='read the result',confidence='medium',reconstruction='A regular local reading is described separately; no singular global theorem is imported.',status='the regular interpretation belongs to standard symplectic reduction, while the notebook does not determine a distinct global open problem.')
add(570,'When Fourier integral kernels form an algebra','P001051','claim',r'Fourier integral operators associated with \(\Lambda\) are denoted \(\mathcal E_\Lambda\); they form a ring because \(\Lambda\circ\Lambda=\Lambda\).',r'Closure under composition requires an admissible operator class: appropriate proper support, a clean canonical fiber product and the corresponding excess/order convention. In the transverse case, properly supported Fourier integral operators compose with summed orders when the canonical projection is proper. An idempotent set-theoretic relation alone does not ensure that arbitrary distribution kernels can be multiplied and integrated.',r'The preexisting transverse theorem is \citet[Theorems~4.2.2--4.2.3]{hormander1971fourier}, published in 1971. The notebook\textquotesingle s characteristic quotient generally calls for its clean-composition extension; it does not specify a complete analytic or complex-microlocal calculus.',('hormander1971fourier',),verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='Support, cleanliness and filtration qualifications are supplied, without identifying the notation with a unique sheaf of microdifferential operators.')
add(570,'The product model: pseudodifferential in one factor, smoothing in the other','P001051','construction',r'For \(X=X_1\times X_2\), the formula is \(\Lambda=\operatorname{diag}(T^*X_1)\times0\); operators are pseudodifferential in \(X_1\), smoothing in \(X_2\). The preceding prose describes covectors normal to \(X_1\), along \(X_2\).',r'The formula and operator description agree with \(\Sigma=T^*X_1\times0_{X_2}\): its covectors annihilate tangent vectors to the \(X_2\) fibers. Its characteristic leaves vary in \(X_2\), and \(\Lambda=\operatorname{diag}(T^*X_1)\times(0_{X_2}\times0_{X_2})\). Here each zero denotes an entire zero section, not a single zero covector at one base point. Product kernels are pseudodifferential in the first variables and smooth in both second-factor variables.',r'The distinction follows directly from the conormal and kernel definitions in \citet[Chapters~II--III]{hormander1971fourier}. The two factor labels in the notebook\textquotesingle s verbal normal-direction description are reversed relative to its formula and operator conclusion.',('hormander1971fourier',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The zero sections are expanded and the normal-direction wording is emended to match the two independent displayed descriptions.')
add(570,'Shmelyov’s conormal embedding condition','P001051','method',r'Shmelyov draws a double fibration \(Z\to X,Y\) and requires the induced map \(N^*Z\to T^*Y\) to be an embedding.',r'For a smooth incidence submanifold \(Z\subset X\times Y\), remove zero covectors and use the signed conormal as the canonical relation of the integral transform \(R:C_c^\infty(X)\to C^\infty(Y)\). Injective immersion of its projection to the data cotangent bundle is the Bolker condition. With appropriate properness and a nonvanishing weight, \(R^*R\) is pseudodifferential and elliptic on visible covectors; complete visibility gives global microlocal ellipticity. This alone does not imply exact injectivity for every smooth weight.',r'\citet[Introduction and Section~2]{homanzhou2017radon} explicitly attributes the FIO and Bolker normal-operator theorem to earlier Guillemin--Sternberg work. Its modern injectivity and stability theorems impose additional analytic or generic hypotheses. Thus the condition in the notes is expository; later stronger results are kept separate.',('homanzhou2017radon',),verification='indirect',match='exact',reconstruction='The incidence embedding, zero-section removal, signs, properness and visibility are specified.',speaker='Shmelyov')
corr('P001050','Speaker heading and catalogue attribution','Catalogue V. L. Ginzburg; notebook V. Ginzburg.','Keep the catalogue form as supplied and flag the probable mathematical identification as Viktor A. Ginzburg.','Scan1050 has only the initial V. The exact adjacent symplectic-geometry publications are by V. A. Ginzburg; this is a provenance inference, not a silently expanded manuscript name.',type='provenance')
corr('P001050','Lagrangian relation in a product cotangent bundle',r'\(\Lambda\subset T^*X\times T^*X\), with no symplectic sign convention.',r'Use the product form \(\omega\oplus(-\omega)\), or equivalently reverse the second covector when treating a kernel conormal.','Scan1050 inspected. Even the diagonal is Lagrangian only for the reversed second symplectic form.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001051','Product example, normal directions',r'Covectors normal to \(X_1\), along \(X_2\), followed by a diagonal in \(T^*X_1\).',r'The formula and operator conclusion require covectors annihilating \(TX_2\), hence \(\Sigma=T^*X_1\times0_{X_2}\).','Scan1051 confirms the verbal factor order and the incompatible displayed formula. A covector normal to the first factor instead has first cotangent component zero and exchanges the two operator roles.')
audit.append('## Record 570\nRussian1050--upper1051 read; both scans inspected, including null-leaf coordinates, product factor labels and Shmelyov double-fibration arrows. Speaker V. only in manuscript; catalogue V.L. conflicts with probable mathematical V.A. attribution, recorded as provenance. Ginzburg full two-page original https://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=faa&option_lang=rus&paperid=1562&what=fullt read, Theorems1--5 and references: 1981MSUpreprint explicitly cited; 1983paper received23December1982. It is not an exact printed version of the idempotent-converse sentence. Guillemin--Sternberg1979 DOI10.2307/2373923 and JSTOR original inaccessible; metadata corroborated by Ginzburg1983 reference1, but no extra bibliography entry falsely labelled full-read. Hormander1971 full author text mirrored by YMSC opened; Theorems4.2.2--4.2.3 read, retaining transversality/properness rather than pretending it proves every clean-excess case. Homan--Zhou full publisher article https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12220-016-9729-4 read Sections1,2,4; its historical attribution supports Bolker priority indirectly. Guillemin1985 line-complex full Numdam article opened but not used for an unrelated general Bolker statement. Previous index1974-356.10 and357.4 are related FIO items, not an identified exact idempotent problem. No C/D assigned to missing regularity choices.')
